  {
    "sha": "78a23c8b3a79e147b8c1ceee152759b8ea6913da",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDc4YTIzYzhiM2E3OWUxNDdiOGMxY2VlZTE1Mjc1OWI4ZWE2OTEzZGE",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T04:47:22Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T04:47:22Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(tools): a tool carries cutting data per workpiece material\n\nA tool gains a per-material table of spindle rpm and feed, keyed by the\nbuilt-in work-material groups. The tool's own rpm/feed stay the running\nnumbers the post reads and the fallback. A row moves them only through\na command, one undo step each: SetWorkMaterial re-seeds the tools with a\nrow and their ops' milling feed, AddTool starts on the document\nmaterial's row, a table edit in ChangeTool loads it, a tool retarget\nand the quick-adds seed the op feed. Nothing moves on load.\n\nThe file key cutting_data is additive and written only when stated, so\na tool without rows saves byte for byte as before (no version step).\nAn unknown material word or a bad row is refused by name. The tool\neditor edits the rows under a collapsed header; MCP add_tool's\ndefinition takes the list.\n\nFixes #890\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
